  "notes": "West leads the ♦K against 6♠. West's diamond lead goes to the ♦A. West has shown at least 5 diamonds and 5 clubs with his unusual no trump. West is likely to be short in spades. South should play a low spade towards the dummy at trick 2.\n\nIf West follows with an honour, the bidding and restricted choice both suggest finessing East for the other honour. When West discards, South must try to take 4 heart tricks for a diamond discard. East will have to follow to all 4 hearts. One possibility is to play West for doubleton ♥J10. South can also pick up the hearts if West has a doubleton heart including the ♥7 and a small heart.\n\nAs there is only 1 way West can hold ♥J10 doubleton and 3 ways he can hold ♥7 doubleton, South should play for the latter holding with West. The ♥8 or ♥9 should be played at trick 3. East covers and South wins.\n\nSouth crosses in clubs and plays North's other middle heart, again covered and won by South. When West's ♥7 drops, the ♥6 is cashed. South crosses again in clubs and plays the ♥Q, pitching the ♦2.\n\nA trump is played from North. East splits his honours and South wins. South's club loser can be ruffed in the dummy. The defense will be held to only 1 trick.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
